
export anchor BoardKeepsItsScrollWhenYouComeBack2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        Planning is a body tab, as spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.description
        says. The Body keeps a task tab "as it was left", as
        spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.description says, but the spec says
        nothing about Planning's scroll, so this is new. Once it's done:
        - Switching to another tab and back shows the board scrolled sideways
          exactly as it was left, and every column scrolled up or down as it was,
          with nothing animating.
        - The same holds when the side panel opens or closes in the meantime: the
          columns keep their own scroll, and only the board's sideways scroll
          moves, as the panel's opening needs.
        - Closing the Planning tab and opening it again, or reloading the window,
          starts the board at its left edge and every column at its top. Scroll
          positions aren't saved with the plan in `order.json`.
        Write this into Planning's layout section first, then make the board match.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            Planning is a body tab, as spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.description
            says. The Body keeps a task tab "as it was left", as
            spec/scope/application/Body.pi#Body.description says, but the spec says
            nothing about Planning's scroll, so this is new. Once it's done:
            - Switching to another tab and back shows the board scrolled sideways
              exactly as it was left, and every column scrolled up or down as it was,
              with nothing animating.
            - The same holds when the side panel opens or closes in the meantime: the
              columns keep their own scroll, and only the board's sideways scroll
              moves, as the panel's opening needs.
            - Closing the Planning tab and opening it again, or reloading the window,
              starts the board at its left edge and every column at its top. Scroll
              positions aren't saved with the plan in `order.json`.
            Write this into Planning's layout section first, then make the board match.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I've added the scroll rules to Planning's layout section in `spec/scope/planning/index.pi`, as a new paragraph right after the scrolling rules. `piton check` reports no problems, `piton build` succeeded, and the compiled reference shows the new text.

            **What the spec now says:**
            - **Coming back to the tab:** while the Planning tab stays open, the board is kept as it was left, as the Body keeps a tab. Switching to another tab and back shows the board scrolled sideways exactly where it was and every column scrolled up or down exactly where it was. This happens at once, with nothing animating, and nothing is reset or scrolled to a card on the way back.
            - **Side panel in the meantime:** if the panel opens or closes while you're away, only the board's sideways scroll changes, as far as the motion section's rules need, and it is already settled when you return. Every column keeps its own scroll.
            - **Closing or reloading:** the scroll belongs to the board and is never saved, in `order.json` or anywhere else. Closing and reopening the Planning tab, or reloading the window, starts the board at its left edge and every column at its top.

            I also fixed a wrapping bug in the paragraph just before it: a line starting with `column:` was being read as a key. That had split the compiled Layout section under a stray "Column" heading, which is now gone.

            **For the code step:** follow `Planning.layout` (the "Layout" section of `.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md`), the new paragraph beginning "The board is kept as it was left". It works together with `Planning.motion` (the side panel's sideways scroll), `Body` (tabs kept as left) and `Planning.saving` (scroll is not part of `order.json`).
            \\\

    sentFrom: BoardKeepsItsScrollWhenYouComeBack

    model: opus
